description:
In public/graph.html, in-progress nodes only get a faint rAF-driven shine sweep that's easy to miss, so they read as un-animated. Add a clearly-visible pulsing blue halo ring via CSS keyframes on .wip nodes.

EXACT CHANGE (already designed — apply verbatim):

Find this block (around line 117-120):
  /* work-in-progress: static blue ring + left-to-right shine sweep on node body */
  #flowsvg g.fn .fhalo { opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; }
  #flowsvg g.fn.wip .fhalo { opacity: .5; stroke: #58a6ff; stroke-width: 1.5; }
  #flowsvg g.fn .fshine-wrap { opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; }

Replace with:
  /* work-in-progress: pulsing blue ring + left-to-right shine sweep on node body */
  #flowsvg g.fn .fhalo { opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; }
  #flowsvg g.fn.wip .fhalo { opacity: 1; stroke: #58a6ff; animation: wipHalo 1.6s ease-in-out infinite; }
  @keyframes wipHalo { 0%,100% { stroke-opacity: .7; stroke-width: 1.5; } 50% { stroke-opacity: .12; stroke-width: 3.5; } }
  #flowsvg g.fn .fshine-wrap { opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; }

Leave the existing shine machinery (runWipShineFrame / syncWipShineLoop / .fshine-wrap) untouched — the halo pulse complements it. Do not touch the focused-halo rule (.focused .fhalo, steady amber) which follows. This is the only change. No tests needed (pure CSS); verify the file parses by confirming the @keyframes wipHalo block is present.

